            {
              "function": "set_components",
              "components": {
                "minecraft:written_book_content": {
                  "title": "001: Torterra Variants",
                  "author": "Professor Sans",
                  "pages": [
                    "\"Journal Entry 001: Tortera Variants\"",
                    "\"I was intrigued when I first heard the rumors: a Grottle’s evolution altered by the sapling it holds. Not unheard of—there are tales of Pokémon changing form based on items or environment—but this? This was different.\"",
                    "{\"text\":\"I gave a Grottle a Mangrove Sapling from the swamps and left it in natural sunlight. By Day 3, it had transformed into a Mangrove Torterra—towering, serene, and somehow... in tune with the wetlands.\n\"}",
                    "{\"text\":\"I've begun cataloging these variant Tortera forms, each deeply bonded to the biome their sapling represents.  So far, I’ve observed signs of change when the pokemon is exposed to:\n- Spruce\n- Dark Oak\n- Birch\n- Oak\n\"}",
                    "{\"text\":\"I theorize the sapling acts as a genetic key, imprinting environmental data into Grottle’s evolution. Less about battle typing—more about ecological resonance.\n\"}",
                    "{\"text\":\"If this proves true, it could reshape how we understand evolution entirely.\n\"}"
                  ]
                }
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
